2025-05-01 03:29:45 - Thread-1459 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant tables / columns and their potential usage
• Match.id: unique identifier for each game (mostly for debugging / sampling).  
• Match.league_id: tells us which league the game belongs to.  
• Match.home_team_api_id / Match.away_team_api_id: identify the two clubs that played.  
• Match.home_team_goal / Match.away_team_goal: needed to decide who won the match.  
• Team.team_api_id: the same id used inside the Match table, allows us to grab the club’s long name.  
• Team.team_long_name: the human‑readable club name we ultimately want to show.  
• League.id: matches Match.league_id, lets us attach the league’s name.  
• League.name: the human‑readable league name we ultimately want to show.

Main conditions / logic that will later be used  
• Home win → home_team_goal  > away_team_goal  
• Away win → away_team_goal  > home_team_goal  
• Draw  → home_team_goal  = away_team_goal (counts 0 wins for both sides).  
• A team must be counted even if it never wins → build a list of every team that ever appeared as home or away inside a league and LEFT JOIN the win totals onto that list.  
• When several teams tie for the fewest wins, return just one (e.g. the lexicographically first club name or the smallest team_api_id).

Below are ten exploratory → increasingly complex SQL snippets (all capped with LIMIT 20 for safety) finishing with one complete solution query.  
Put each statement in its own run block.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at the list of leagues
SELECT id, name
FROM League
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Peek at the list of teams
SELECT team_api_id, team_long_name, team_short_name
FROM Team
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect a few matches: who played, goals, league
SELECT id, league_id, season,
       home_team_api_id, away_team_api_id,
       home_team_goal, away_team_goal
FROM Match
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Tag each sampled match with who (if anyone) won
SELECT id,
       CASE
           WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN home_team_api_id
           WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN away_team_api_id
           ELSE NULL
       END        AS winner_team_api_id
FROM Match
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Count home‑side wins per team (any league)
SELECT home_team_api_id                        AS team_api_id,
       COUNT(*)                                AS home_wins
FROM   Match
WHERE  home_team_goal > away_team_goal
GROUP  BY home_team_api_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Count away‑side wins per team (any league)
SELECT away_team_api_id                        AS team_api_id,
       COUNT(*)                                AS away_wins
FROM   Match
WHERE  away_team_goal > home_team_goal
GROUP  BY away_team_api_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Combine home + away to get total wins per team *per league*
SELECT league_id,
       team_api_id,
       SUM(wins) AS total_wins
FROM (
        -- home wins
        SELECT league_id,
               home_team_api_id AS team_api_id,
               CASE WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS wins
        FROM   Match

        UNION ALL

        -- away wins
        SELECT league_id,
               away_team_api_id AS team_api_id,
               CASE WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS wins
        FROM   Match
)
GROUP  BY league_id, team_api_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Show every team that ever appeared in a league and attach win totals (0 if none)
WITH teams_in_league AS (
        SELECT league_id, home_team_api_id AS team_api_id FROM Match
        UNION
        SELECT league_id, away_team_api_id FROM Match
),
wins AS (
        SELECT league_id,
               team_api_id,
               SUM(wins) AS total_wins
        FROM (
                SELECT league_id,
                       home_team_api_id AS team_api_id,
                       CASE WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS wins
                FROM   Match
                UNION ALL
                SELECT league_id,
                       away_team_api_id AS team_api_id,
                       CASE WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS wins
                FROM   Match
        )
        GROUP BY league_id, team_api_id
)
SELECT til.league_id,
       til.team_api_id,
       COALESCE(w.total_wins, 0) AS wins
FROM   teams_in_league AS til
LEFT JOIN wins              AS w
       ON w.league_id   = til.league_id
      AND w.team_api_id = til.team_api_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Minimum number of wins found inside each league
WITH wins_with_zeros AS (
        /* see CTE from previous query */
        SELECT til.league_id,
               til.team_api_id,
               COALESCE(w.total_wins, 0) AS wins
        FROM (
                SELECT league_id, home_team_api_id AS team_api_id FROM Match
                UNION
                SELECT league_id, away_team_api_id FROM Match
        ) AS til
        LEFT JOIN (
                SELECT league_id,
                       team_api_id,
                       SUM(wins) AS total_wins
                FROM (
                        SELECT league_id,
                               home_team_api_id AS team_api_id,
                               CASE WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS wins
                        FROM   Match
                        UNION ALL
                        SELECT league_id,
                               away_team_api_id AS team_api_id,
                               CASE WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS wins
                        FROM   Match
                )
                GROUP BY league_id, team_api_id
        ) AS w
        ON  w.league_id   = til.league_id
        AND w.team_api_id = til.team_api_id
)
SELECT league_id, MIN(wins) AS fewest_wins
FROM   wins_with_zeros
GROUP  BY league_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER: for every league return ONE team that has the fewest total wins (0‑wins included)
WITH teams_in_league AS (
        SELECT league_id, home_team_api_id AS team_api_id FROM Match
        UNION
        SELECT league_id, away_team_api_id FROM Match
),
team_wins AS (
        -- home wins
        SELECT league_id,
               home_team_api_id AS team_api_id,
               SUM(CASE WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS wins
        FROM   Match
        GROUP  BY league_id, home_team_api_id

        UNION ALL

        -- away wins
        SELECT league_id,
               away_team_api_id AS team_api_id,
               SUM(CASE WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS wins
        FROM   Match
        GROUP  BY league_id, away_team_api_id
),
total_wins AS (
        SELECT league_id, team_api_id, SUM(wins) AS wins
        FROM   team_wins
        GROUP  BY league_id, team_api_id
),
wins_with_zeros AS (
        SELECT til.league_id,
               til.team_api_id,
               COALESCE(tw.wins, 0) AS wins
        FROM   teams_in_league AS til
        LEFT JOIN total_wins    AS tw
               ON  tw.league_id   = til.league_id
               AND tw.team_api_id = til.team_api_id
),
fewest AS (
        SELECT league_id,
               MIN(wins) AS min_wins
        FROM   wins_with_zeros
        GROUP  BY league_id
)
SELECT l.name                      AS league_name,
       MIN(t.team_long_name)       AS team_with_fewest_wins,
       f.min_wins                  AS total_wins
FROM   wins_with_zeros  AS w
JOIN   fewest          AS f  ON f.league_id = w.league_id
                             AND f.min_wins = w.wins
JOIN   League          AS l  ON l.id        = w.league_id
JOIN   Team            AS t  ON t.team_api_id = w.team_api_id
GROUP  BY w.league_id
ORDER  BY l.name
LIMIT 20;
```

Query‑10 produces exactly one row per league, listing the single team that owns the lowest number of victories across all recorded seasons (ties are broken by simply taking the alphabetically first club name via MIN()).
